![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Using `setHours` on a Date object created from an ISO string (which defaults to UTC) can lead to unexpected results depending on the system's local timezone. For example, if the system is in a negative offset (like UTC-5), `new Date('2024-03-01')` (UTC midnight) becomes 7 PM on the previous day in local time; `setHours(0)` then shifts it to the start of that previous day. Using `setUTCHours` ensures consistent filtering regardless of the server's timezone.

```suggestion
function toStartOfDay(d: Date): Date {
  const r = new Date(d);
  r.setUTCHours(0, 0, 0, 0);
  return r;
}

function toEndOfDay(d: Date): Date {
  const r = new Date(d);
  r.setUTCHours(23, 59, 59, 999);
  return r;
}
```

